## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of fast, branchless bit tricks and constant-time bit-scanning utilities common in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- Isolating least-significant 1: v = n & -n
- De Bruijn-based indexing: map a 5-bit index to a trailing-zero count
- Handling edge case n == 0 (return 32)
- Static lookup table of 32 entries

## Code Example
```c
uint32_t countTrailingZeros32(uint32_t n){
  static const int debruijn[32] = {0,1,28,2,29,14,24,3,30,22,20,15,25,17,4,8,31,27,13,23,21,19,16,7,26,12,18,9,6,11,5,10};
  if(n == 0) return 32;
  uint32_t v = n & -n;
  return debruijn[(v * 0x077CB531) >> 27];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs?
- What are alternatives that avoid a static table, e.g., using binary search on bit groups?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a constant-time popcount-style operation using only masks and shifts, simulating a common bitmap diff in games or databases.

## Key Concepts
- Hamming weight via parallel reduction
- XOR to identify differences
- Mask sequences for bit counting

## Code Example
```cpp
int hammingDistance64(uint64 a, uint64 b){
  uint64 x = a ^ b;
  x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) + ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL);
  x = (x & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL);
  x = (x & 0x0000ffff0000ffffULL) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000ffff0000ffffULL);
  x = (x & 0x00000000ffffffffULL) + ((x >> 32) & 0x00000000ffffffffULL);
  return (int)x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt the approach for 128-bit inputs?
- What are the trade-offs vs builtin popcount on modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary bit interactions and implement constant-time checks using only bitwise ops. 

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise shifts and masks across multiword values
- Detecting adjacent bits within each 64-bit half and across the 64-bit boundary
- Branchless boolean results for interview clarity

## Code Example
```javascript
int hasAdjacentOnes128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo & (x.lo << 1);
  uint64_t b = x.hi & (x.hi << 1);
  int cross = ((x.lo >> 63) & 1) & (x.hi & 1);
  return (a | b | cross) != 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to detect a run of exactly k adjacent ones? 
- How would you implement a similar check for 256-bit values represented as two uint128s?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests facility with non-trivial bit tricks on 128-bit values, without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Per-byte masking and bit propagation
- Operating on 128-bit lanes with fixed-width shifts
- Constructing and applying 128-bit constants

## Code Example
```cpp
uint128 perByteMSB128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 t = x;
  t |= t >> 1;
  t |= t >> 2;
  t |= t >> 4;
  const uint128 msbMask = 0x80808080808080808080808080808080;
  return t & msbMask;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test edge lanes across all 16 bytes?
- Could this be vectorized with SIMD intrinsics if available?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit interleaving without loops, a common low-level optimization in graphics/encoding.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (Morton order)
- Mask-and-shift interleaving
- 128-bit composition from two 64-bit halves

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudocode outline (no branches)
uint128 interleaveBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  uint64_t sLo = spreadBy2(lo); // using masks 0x5555.., 0x3333.., etc.
  uint64_t sHi = spreadBy2(hi);
  r.lo = sLo;
  r.hi = sHi << 1;
  return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests precise bit permutation across 128-bit boundary without loops. Requires careful cross-half carry handling when shifting hi/lo.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value as {hi, lo}
- Masks for even/odd bit positions across both halves
- Cross-half carry during shifts between lo and hi

## Code Example

```javascript
// Outline (not full implementation)
uint128 swapAdjacentBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 evenMask = {0x5555555555555555ULL, 0x5555555555555555ULL};
  uint128 oddMask  = {0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL, 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL};
  uint64_t lo_e = x.lo & evenMask.lo; uint64_t hi_e = x.hi & evenMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_o = x.lo & oddMask.lo;  uint64_t hi_o = x.hi & oddMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_left  = (lo_e << 1);
  uint64_t hi_left  = (hi_e << 1) | (lo_e >> 63);
  uint64_t lo_right = (lo_o >> 1);
  uint64_t hi_right = (hi_o >> 1);
  uint128 res;
  res.lo = lo_left | lo_right;
  res.hi = hi_left | hi_right;
  // handle cross-half carry from lo_o/hi_o if needed
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to arbitrary bit widths or detect overflow across the 128-bit boundary?
- How would you unit-test edge cases: all zeros, all ones, alternating patterns, and patterns crossing the boundary?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit arithmetic, two's-complement negation, and branchless masking, all crucial for low-level optimizations in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo limbs
- Two's-complement negation across limb boundaries
- Branchless bit masking using bitwise operations
- Edge cases: x==0 and bit in hi vs lo limb

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = (~x.lo) + 1;
  uint64_t hiNeg = (~x.hi) + (loNeg == 0);
  uint128 mask;
  mask.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  mask.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  return mask;
}
```

## Examples
- Input: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}` → Output: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}`
- Input: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}` → Output: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}`
